================================================================================
Gadgetbridge
================================================================================

The Tracks mobile app (mobile/) incorporates source code from Gadgetbridge,
which provides the reverse-engineered Bluetooth protocols used to communicate
with fitness watches.

    Gadgetbridge
    Copyright (C) 2015-2026 Andreas Shimokawa, Carsten Pfeiffer, Daniele
    Gobbetti, José Rebelo, and the Gadgetbridge contributors
    https://codeberg.org/Freeyourgadget/Gadgetbridge

    Licensed under the GNU Affero General Public License v3.0 or later.

Vendored from Gadgetbridge 0.93.0 (versionCode 252), under
mobile/device-garmin/src/main/ and mobile/fit-codegen/src/main/. The exact file
list, and — more usefully — what is deliberately left behind and why, is in
mobile/vendor/gadgetbridge.manifest. Refresh with
mobile/vendor/pull-gadgetbridge.sh.

mobile/fit-codegen/ holds Gadgetbridge's FIT message code generator. It is a
build tool rather than app code and never reaches the APK — only the Java it
emits does. It is not optional: upstream checks in only four FIT message
classes and generates the other ~190 from Garmin's profile JSON at build time.

The vendored sources keep their original package names, so that taking
upstream's later fixes stays a file copy. Three files carry deliberate edits,
each recorded as a patch in mobile/vendor/patches/ and re-applied on every
refresh — the pull fails loudly if one no longer applies:

  0001  CommunicatorV2: removes Gadgetbridge's own activity-sample store.
        Tracks' samples reach its server inside the uploaded FIT files, so a
        second store would be a second source of truth.
  0002  GBTarFile: uses java.util.Arrays instead of a shaded Bouncy Castle
        copy, for a single array copy.
  0003  MlrCommunicator: rewrites one private record as a plain final class.
        Not a preference — desugaring a record for minSdk 26 requires a D8
        global synthetic, and AGP's library dexing transform cannot emit one,
        so the build fails outright.

Most vendored files carry no copyright header of their own; their licensing is
declared in bulk in REUSE.toml rather than by editing them.

mobile/device-garmin/src/main/res/values/gadgetbridge_*.xml are generated at
vendoring time from upstream's strings.xml by vendor/generate-gb-resources.py.
The ~190 drawable entries there are aliases to one placeholder icon of our own:
Gadgetbridge's artwork is under a separate licence (see below) and is not
vendored, and Tracks draws its own sport icons.

Gadgetbridge's user interface, its greenDAO database, and its FIT importer are
NOT vendored: Tracks sends raw FIT bytes to its own server and lets the server
parse them, so a second parser and a second store would be two more things to
keep in step.

Gadgetbridge's own artwork (see LICENSE.artwork in the upstream repository) is
NOT covered by the AGPL and is not vendored here.

================================================================================
BRouter
================================================================================

The Tracks mobile app also incorporates BRouter's routing engine, so that a
phone can plan a trail route with no network at all.

    BRouter
    Copyright (c) 2014-2026 BRouter contributors
    https://github.com/abrensch/brouter

    Licensed under the MIT License.

Vendored from BRouter v1.7.9 under mobile/routing-brouter/src/main/java/btools/,
with the file list and the reasons for the carve-outs in
mobile/vendor/brouter.manifest. Refresh with mobile/vendor/pull-brouter.sh. As
with Gadgetbridge, the sources keep their original package names and carry no
per-file headers; their licensing is declared in bulk in REUSE.toml.

The server runs the same project as a separate service (the `brouter`
container) for the browser and for phones with signal. Both ends therefore
weigh trails identically and return the same GeoJSON, which is the point.

BRouter's HTTP server, its own Android app, and its rd5 map-creator are NOT
vendored. The .brf profiles and lookups.dat are data rather than code and are
not vendored either: they must match the rd5 segments, so the phone fetches
them from its Tracks server.

================================================================================
react-muscle-highlighter
================================================================================

Both clients draw the anatomical muscle diagram with artwork from
react-muscle-highlighter.

    react-muscle-highlighter 1.2.0
    Copyright (c) 2024 My Muscle Contributors
    https://github.com/soroojshehryar/react-muscle-highlighter

    Licensed under the MIT License.

The web app uses the React component as published. The mobile app cannot — it
is a React component and Compose is not React — but its artwork is not React
at all: the bodies are plain SVG path strings in dist/esm/assets/body*.js,
which Compose parses natively. So mobile/core/.../spec/BodyArtData.kt is those
path strings transcribed into Kotlin by frontend/scripts/build-body-art.mjs.
Same paths, same part slugs, same licence — one drawing rendered two ways,
rather than a second body model that agrees with the first for now.

That generated file carries both copyrights and both licences, because it
contains both: the artwork is MIT and belongs to its authors, the Kotlin
around it is ours.

Only the four body assets are taken. The React component, its SVG wrappers,
and its hover and press behaviour are not — the phone draws and hit-tests the
paths itself.

